1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,500
Should public money help pay for election campaigns?

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:06,700
That is the question behind California Proposition four.

3
00:00:06,993 --> 00:00:10,293
Proposition four would let more governments offer public

4
00:00:10,293 --> 00:00:10,833
financing.

5
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:14,253
It does not automatically launch a program or

6
00:00:14,253 --> 00:00:15,433
send money to candidates.

7
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:18,760
Candidates would have to demonstrate broad support in

8
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:20,740
their district and accept spending limits.

9
00:00:21,027 --> 00:00:23,607
But other public funds spent on campaigns still

10
00:00:23,607 --> 00:00:25,087
have an opportunity cost.

11
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:27,180
A voter favoring reform might say:

12
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:30,540
I want candidates with community support to compete,

13
00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:32,740
even without wealthy backers.

14
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:35,540
Another voter might ask: should taxpayers pay for

15
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:36,520
political campaigns?

16
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:40,360
They may dislike funding candidates they oppose, or

17
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:42,340
prefer other uses for public money.

18
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:45,120
A no vote keeps the general ban and

19
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:46,280
existing exceptions.

20
00:00:46,573 --> 00:00:48,333
Visit our website for the fuller explanation,

21
00:00:48,973 --> 00:00:51,373
sources, and official voter guide.
